export default JSON.parse("{\"0.1.1\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.1\",\"bridge\":\"This is where the course begins. Before any chart or order, you need one idea: where a price comes from. Here you run a small auction and watch buyers and sellers make the price.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"LABX, this course's practice instrument, last traded at 100.00. A buyer arrives who wants 200 [[unit|units]], or pieces of LABX, right now, and only a few sellers are offering. Where will the next trades print?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"below\",\"label\":\"Below 100.00\"},{\"id\":\"around\",\"label\":\"Around 100.00\"},{\"id\":\"above\",\"label\":\"Above 100.00\"}],\"answer\":\"above\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. The hurried buyer has to take the sellers' prices, one after another, so the next trades print above 100.00.\",\"concept\":\"market-as-auction\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"scene\",\"title\":\"Where a price comes from\",\"scene\":\"auction-intro\",\"chapters\":[{\"title\":\"Every point is a trade\",\"caption\":\"Every price on a chart is a trade that already happened: a buyer and a seller agreed on that number. Nobody sets the price; it comes out of the [[order|orders]].\"},{\"title\":\"Two sides\",\"caption\":\"A [[market]] is where orders to buy meet orders to sell. Each buyer pays **at most** their price; each seller accepts **at least** theirs. Five of each here, one [[unit]] apiece.\"},{\"title\":\"Two queues\",\"caption\":\"Sort them by price and you get two queues: the keenest buyer at the top, the cheapest seller at the bottom.\"},{\"title\":\"When can they trade?\",\"caption\":\"Each order accepts a range: a buyer anything up to their price, a seller anything from theirs. Where the two ranges overlap, they can trade.\"},{\"title\":\"One price for everyone\",\"caption\":\"In an auction, everyone trades at one price. Which price lets the most units change hands? You'll find it on the next screen.\"}],\"stops\":[{\"prompt\":\"This buyer pays at most 99.80. This seller wants at least 100.25. Can they trade?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"yes\",\"label\":\"Yes, they can meet in the middle\",\"feedback\":\"There's no middle to meet in: every price the seller accepts is above what the buyer will pay.\"},{\"id\":\"no\",\"label\":\"No\",\"feedback\":\"99.80 is below 100.25, so no price suits both of them.\"}],\"answer\":\"no\"}],\"concept\":\"market-as-auction\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Run the auction\",\"prompt\":\"Everyone trades at one price. Slide it: where do the most units change hands?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":99.7,\"max\":100.3,\"step\":0.05,\"start\":99.7,\"answer\":100,\"tolerance\":0,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":99.7,\"to\":99.7,\"text\":\"At 99.70, 5 buyers will pay that much and 1 seller will accept it: 1 unit trades. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":99.75,\"to\":99.75,\"text\":\"At 99.75, 5 buyers will pay that much and 1 seller will accept it: 1 unit trades. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":99.8,\"to\":99.8,\"text\":\"At 99.80, 5 buyers will pay that much and 1 seller will accept it: 1 unit trades. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":99.85,\"to\":99.85,\"text\":\"At 99.85, 4 buyers will pay that much and 2 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":99.9,\"to\":99.9,\"text\":\"At 99.90, 4 buyers will pay that much and 2 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":99.95,\"to\":99.95,\"text\":\"At 99.95, 4 buyers will pay that much and 2 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":100,\"to\":100,\"text\":\"At 100.00, 3 buyers will pay that much and 3 sellers will accept it: 3 units trade. No other price clears more.\"},{\"from\":100.05,\"to\":100.05,\"text\":\"At 100.05, 2 buyers will pay that much and 4 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":100.1,\"to\":100.1,\"text\":\"At 100.10, 2 buyers will pay that much and 4 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":100.15,\"to\":100.15,\"text\":\"At 100.15, 2 buyers will pay that much and 4 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":100.2,\"to\":100.2,\"text\":\"At 100.20, 2 buyers will pay that much and 4 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":100.25,\"to\":100.25,\"text\":\"At 100.25, 1 buyer will pay that much and 5 sellers will accept it: 1 unit trades. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":100.3,\"to\":100.3,\"text\":\"At 100.30, 1 buyer will pay that much and 5 sellers will accept it: 1 unit trades. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"}]},\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100}],\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":100.3,\"label\":\"BUY 100.30\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":100.2,\"label\":\"BUY 100.20\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":100,\"label\":\"BUY 100.00\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":99.95,\"label\":\"BUY 99.95\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":99.8,\"label\":\"BUY 99.80\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":99.7,\"label\":\"SELL 99.70\",\"role\":\"down\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":99.85,\"label\":\"SELL 99.85\",\"role\":\"down\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":100,\"label\":\"SELL 100.00\",\"role\":\"down\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":100.05,\"label\":\"SELL 100.05\",\"role\":\"down\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":100.25,\"label\":\"SELL 100.25\",\"role\":\"down\"}],\"range\":[99.6,100.4],\"height\":320,\"describe\":\"An order book. Left column, buyers paying at most 100.30, 100.20, 100.00, 99.95 and 99.80. Right column, sellers accepting at least 99.70, 99.85, 100.00, 100.05 and 100.25. A dash in the middle marks the last trade, 100.00.\"},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"At 100.00, three buyers and three sellers agree: 3 units trade.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":100,\"role\":\"concept\",\"chip\":true}]}]},\"reveal\":\"At each price, count the buyers who would pay it and the sellers who would accept it. Units traded is the smaller count. At 100.00 both counts are 3; at 99.95 or 100.05 only 2 units can trade. That price becomes the last trade.\",\"concept\":\"market-as-auction\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"What's left after the auction\",\"text\":\"The spread you'll pay on every LABX trade is this gap: the space between the best buyer and the best seller still waiting.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100}],\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":99.95,\"label\":\"BUY 99.95\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":99.8,\"label\":\"BUY 99.80\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":100.05,\"label\":\"SELL 100.05\",\"role\":\"down\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":100.25,\"label\":\"SELL 100.25\",\"role\":\"down\"}],\"range\":[99.6,100.4],\"height\":320,\"describe\":\"After the auction: buyers left at 99.95 and 99.80, sellers left at 100.05 and 100.25. A dash in the middle marks the last trade, 100.00.\"},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"Three units traded at 100.00. Still waiting: buyers at 99.95 and 99.80, sellers at 100.05 and 100.25.\"},{\"text\":\"The best [[bid]], 99.95, is the highest buyer left. The best [[ask]], 100.05, is the lowest seller.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":99.95,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":100.05,\"role\":\"neutral\"}]},{\"text\":\"The gap, 0.10, is the [[spread]]. Its middle, 100.00, is the mid that LABX charts draw.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"bracket\",\"from\":99.95,\"to\":100.05,\"index\":1,\"label\":\"SPREAD 0.10\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":100,\"role\":\"neutral\"}]}]}},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"An eager buyer\",\"prompt\":\"A new buyer wants 2 units now and will pay at most 100.20. Sellers wait at 100.05 and 100.25. Where does the last price print?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"same\",\"label\":\"It stays at 100.00\",\"feedback\":\"A trade needs a seller's price. The cheapest seller left asks 100.05, so that's where the next trade prints.\"},{\"id\":\"ask\",\"label\":\"100.05\",\"feedback\":\"Right. One unit trades at the best ask, 100.05, and that becomes the last price.\"},{\"id\":\"limit\",\"label\":\"100.20\",\"feedback\":\"100.20 is the buyer's limit, not a seller's price. The trade happens at a price both accept: 100.05.\"},{\"id\":\"next\",\"label\":\"100.25\",\"feedback\":\"The next seller wants 100.25, above the buyer's 100.20 limit, so no trade happens there.\"}],\"answer\":\"ask\"},\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100},{\"o\":100,\"h\":100,\"l\":100,\"c\":100}],\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":99.95,\"label\":\"BUY 99.95\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":99.8,\"label\":\"BUY 99.80\",\"role\":\"up\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":100.05,\"label\":\"SELL 100.05\",\"role\":\"down\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":2,\"price\":100.25,\"label\":\"SELL 100.25\",\"role\":\"down\"},{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":0,\"price\":100.2,\"label\":\"NEW BUY 100.20 ×2\",\"role\":\"accent\"}],\"range\":[99.6,100.4],\"height\":320,\"describe\":\"The book after the auction, plus a new buyer for 2 units at up to 100.20. Sellers wait at 100.05 and 100.25.\"},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"One unit trades at 100.05, the best ask. The last price rises to 100.05.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":1,\"price\":100.05,\"label\":\"LAST 100.05\",\"role\":\"accent\"}]},{\"text\":\"The next seller wants 100.25, above the limit. The second unit waits as a bid at 100.20.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":100.2,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":100.25,\"role\":\"neutral\"}]}]},\"reveal\":\"The eager buyer took the seller's price, so the last price rose from 100.00 to 100.05. The unfilled unit now waits as the best bid, 100.20, against the best ask, 100.25.\",\"concept\":\"market-as-auction\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Price moves when one side is in more of a hurry than the other. Nobody sets it; orders do.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Your turn: a new book\",\"prompt\":\"One unit per order. Buyers pay at most 50.40, 50.30, 50.20 and 50.10. Sellers accept at least 50.00, 50.10, 50.20 and 50.50. Set the price where the most units change hands.\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":50,\"max\":50.5,\"step\":0.05,\"start\":50,\"answer\":50.2,\"tolerance\":0,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":50,\"to\":50,\"text\":\"At 50.00, 4 buyers will pay that much and 1 seller will accept it: 1 unit trades. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":50.05,\"to\":50.05,\"text\":\"At 50.05, 4 buyers will pay that much and 1 seller will accept it: 1 unit trades. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":50.1,\"to\":50.1,\"text\":\"At 50.10, 4 buyers will pay that much and 2 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":50.15,\"to\":50.15,\"text\":\"At 50.15, 3 buyers will pay that much and 2 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Sellers are the short side here: try a higher price.\"},{\"from\":50.2,\"to\":50.2,\"text\":\"At 50.20, 3 buyers will pay that much and 3 sellers will accept it: 3 units trade. No other price clears more.\"},{\"from\":50.25,\"to\":50.25,\"text\":\"At 50.25, 2 buyers will pay that much and 3 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":50.3,\"to\":50.3,\"text\":\"At 50.30, 2 buyers will pay that much and 3 sellers will accept it: 2 units trade. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":50.35,\"to\":50.35,\"text\":\"At 50.35, 1 buyer will pay that much and 3 sellers will accept it: 1 unit trades. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":50.4,\"to\":50.4,\"text\":\"At 50.40, 1 buyer will pay that much and 3 sellers will accept it: 1 unit trades. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":50.45,\"to\":50.45,\"text\":\"At 50.45, 0 buyers will pay that much and 3 sellers will accept it: 0 units trade. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"},{\"from\":50.5,\"to\":50.5,\"text\":\"At 50.50, 0 buyers will pay that much and 4 sellers will accept it: 0 units trade. Buyers are the short side here: try a lower price.\"}]},\"reveal\":\"50.20: three buyers will pay it and three sellers will accept it, so 3 units trade. At 50.10 or 50.30 only 2 can. Count both sides, take the smaller.\",\"concept\":\"market-as-auction\"}],\"check\":[\"q-0.1.1-1\",\"q-0.1.1-2\",\"q-0.1.1-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"A price is where a buyer and a seller last agreed. Nobody sets it; orders do.\",\"The auction price is where the most units trade: count buyers and sellers willing, take the smaller.\",\"When buyers are in more of a hurry, they take sellers' prices and the price rises.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 0.1.2: who those buyers and sellers are, and why each of them trades.\"}},\"0.1.2\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.2\",\"bridge\":\"In 0.1.1 you ran an auction and saw a price come out of buyers' and sellers' orders. Here you meet the people behind those orders, and why the one across from you trades.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Every time you buy 50 LABX, someone sells you 50. Who is most likely on the other side?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"bear\",\"label\":\"Someone who thinks price will fall\"},{\"id\":\"dealer\",\"label\":\"A dealer or market maker who earns the spread\"},{\"id\":\"unknown\",\"label\":\"Can't tell from the chart\"}]},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. Any of the three can be true. In retail FX and CFDs it's often a dealer or the broker itself, but the chart never shows who. 'Can't tell from the chart' is the honest reading.\",\"concept\":\"market-participants\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Two sides to every trade\",\"text\":\"When you buy, your [[counterparty]] sells. They aren't necessarily wrong. They may have a different horizon, a different need or different information. A pension fund selling to rebalance and you buying for a quick rise can both be acting sensibly.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Who is in the market\",\"text\":\"A [[hedger]] reduces a risk it already carries. Investors hold for years. Institutions move huge sums, often with algorithms. A [[market-maker]] quotes both sides and earns the spread. A [[speculator]] trades for a price move. Central banks act for policy, not profit. A [[retail-trader]] is an individual trading their own money, like you.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Who trades for what\",\"prompt\":\"Match each reason to the participant who trades for it.\",\"columns\":[\"Reason\",\"Participant\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Locks in next year's fuel cost\",\"right\":\"Hedger\"},{\"left\":\"Earns the spread, ends the day flat (holding nothing)\",\"right\":\"Market maker\"},{\"left\":\"Holds for decades for dividends and growth\",\"right\":\"Investor\"},{\"left\":\"Buys because it expects a rise this week\",\"right\":\"Speculator\"},{\"left\":\"Splits a huge order over the day to avoid moving the price\",\"right\":\"Institution\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. Same market, five different reasons to trade. None of them needs you to be wrong for them to be right.\",\"concept\":\"market-participants\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Different worries\",\"text\":\"Institutions worry about their own size: a huge order can push the price against them, so they split it up. Retail traders face different problems: costs, and their own behaviour. Neither side is a villain. But the firms across from you trade full time, with faster tools and lower costs.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Who hunted the stop?\",\"prompt\":\"A trader says: 'They hunted my stop,' the order that closed their trade at a preset loss. Who is 'they'?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"watcher\",\"label\":\"A big trader watching my account\",\"feedback\":\"Nothing on a chart shows who traded or why. Blaming a watcher can't be checked, so it teaches you nothing.\"},{\"id\":\"nobody\",\"label\":\"Nobody in particular: stops gather at obvious levels\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Many traders put stops in the same obvious places, so a move through that level fills them all.\"},{\"id\":\"app\",\"label\":\"The app\",\"feedback\":\"In MARKET//LAB, prices come from a simulation that can't see your orders. Stops get hit because price reaches them.\"}],\"answer\":\"nobody\"},\"reveal\":\"The market isn't a person. Stops do tend to gather just beyond obvious levels, so a move there fills many at once. But nobody's intent can be read from a chart. The useful question is whether your stop sat where everyone else's did.\",\"concept\":\"market-participants\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"When you trade, you trade against people and firms who do this full time, often with better tools and lower costs. Plan with that in mind.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"hedger\",\"label\":\"Hedger\"},{\"id\":\"investor\",\"label\":\"Investor\"},{\"id\":\"institution\",\"label\":\"Institution\"},{\"id\":\"market-maker\",\"label\":\"Market maker\"},{\"id\":\"speculator\",\"label\":\"Speculator\"},{\"id\":\"central-bank\",\"label\":\"Central bank\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"airline\",\"text\":\"An airline buys oil futures to fix next year's fuel cost.\",\"answer\":\"hedger\",\"correct\":\"Hedger. The airline already faces a fuel bill. The futures lock in its cost, whatever oil does next.\",\"rationales\":{\"investor\":\"An investor holds for years, for income and growth. Here the airline already faces a fuel bill and uses futures to lock in its cost.\",\"institution\":\"An institution moves very large orders for a fund or its clients. Here the airline already faces a fuel bill and uses futures to lock in its cost.\",\"market-maker\":\"A market maker quotes a bid and an ask and earns the spread. Here the airline already faces a fuel bill and uses futures to lock in its cost.\",\"speculator\":\"The airline isn't betting on oil. It already carries the fuel risk and trades to remove it, not to profit from a move.\",\"central-bank\":\"A central bank trades for a policy goal, not for profit. Here the airline already faces a fuel bill and uses futures to lock in its cost.\"}},{\"id\":\"pension\",\"text\":\"A pension fund buys 2 million shares over one day, with an algorithm that splits the order into small pieces.\",\"answer\":\"institution\",\"correct\":\"Institution. An order that big would move the price, so the fund splits it to keep its own impact small.\",\"rationales\":{\"hedger\":\"A hedger trades to reduce a risk it already has. Here a fund splits a huge order so that its own size doesn't move the price.\",\"investor\":\"It may well hold for years. What sets this card apart is the size: 2 million shares, split up so the order doesn't move the price.\",\"market-maker\":\"A market maker quotes a bid and an ask and earns the spread. Here a fund splits a huge order so that its own size doesn't move the price.\",\"speculator\":\"A speculator takes a risk to profit from a price move. Here a fund splits a huge order so that its own size doesn't move the price.\",\"central-bank\":\"A central bank trades for a policy goal, not for profit. Here a fund splits a huge order so that its own size doesn't move the price.\"}},{\"id\":\"quotes\",\"text\":\"A firm quotes a bid and an ask on LABX all day and ends the day flat.\",\"answer\":\"market-maker\",\"correct\":\"Market maker. It buys at its bid, sells at its ask and earns the spread, without betting on a direction.\",\"rationales\":{\"hedger\":\"A hedger trades to reduce a risk it already has. Here the firm quotes both sides all day and ends with no position.\",\"investor\":\"An investor holds for years, for income and growth. Here the firm quotes both sides all day and ends with no position.\",\"institution\":\"An institution moves very large orders for a fund or its clients. Here the firm quotes both sides all day and ends with no position.\",\"speculator\":\"A speculator takes a risk to profit from a price move. Here the firm quotes both sides all day and ends with no position.\",\"central-bank\":\"A central bank trades for a policy goal, not for profit. Here the firm quotes both sides all day and ends with no position.\"}},{\"id\":\"you\",\"text\":\"You buy LABX because you expect a rise this week.\",\"answer\":\"speculator\",\"correct\":\"Speculator. You accept a risk for a price move. Every trade you'll place in this course is speculation.\",\"rationales\":{\"hedger\":\"A hedger trades to reduce a risk it already has. Here you buy because you expect a rise: a bet on the move itself.\",\"investor\":\"An investor holds for years, for income and growth. Here you buy because you expect a rise: a bet on the move itself.\",\"institution\":\"An institution moves very large orders for a fund or its clients. Here you buy because you expect a rise: a bet on the move itself.\",\"market-maker\":\"A market maker quotes a bid and an ask and earns the spread. Here you buy because you expect a rise: a bet on the move itself.\",\"central-bank\":\"A central bank trades for a policy goal, not for profit. Here you buy because you expect a rise: a bet on the move itself.\"}},{\"id\":\"saver\",\"text\":\"A saver adds 200.00 to an index fund every month.\",\"answer\":\"investor\",\"correct\":\"Investor. Small regular amounts, held for years: the return comes from the market, not from timing it.\",\"rationales\":{\"hedger\":\"A hedger trades to reduce a risk it already has. Here a saver buys a little every month and holds, whatever the price does.\",\"institution\":\"An institution moves very large orders for a fund or its clients. Here a saver buys a little every month and holds, whatever the price does.\",\"market-maker\":\"A market maker quotes a bid and an ask and earns the spread. Here a saver buys a little every month and holds, whatever the price does.\",\"speculator\":\"A speculator takes a risk to profit from a price move. Here a saver buys a little every month and holds, whatever the price does.\",\"central-bank\":\"A central bank trades for a policy goal, not for profit. Here a saver buys a little every month and holds, whatever the price does.\"}},{\"id\":\"policy\",\"text\":\"A central bank sells its own currency to slow its rise.\",\"answer\":\"central-bank\",\"correct\":\"Central bank. It trades for a policy goal, the exchange rate, not for profit.\",\"rationales\":{\"hedger\":\"A hedger trades to reduce a risk it already has. Here a central bank trades its own currency to steer the exchange rate.\",\"investor\":\"An investor holds for years, for income and growth. Here a central bank trades its own currency to steer the exchange rate.\",\"institution\":\"An institution moves very large orders for a fund or its clients. Here a central bank trades its own currency to steer the exchange rate.\",\"market-maker\":\"A market maker quotes a bid and an ask and earns the spread. Here a central bank trades its own currency to steer the exchange rate.\",\"speculator\":\"A speculator takes a risk to profit from a price move. Here a central bank trades its own currency to steer the exchange rate.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Six trades, six reasons. Read each card and name who is trading.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":6},\"correct\":\"All six named. Same market, six reasons to trade, and the chart shows none of them.\",\"hints\":[\"Ask why they trade: to remove a risk, to earn the spread, to bet on a move, or for a policy goal?\",\"Size and method point to an institution; small monthly amounts held for years point to an investor.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":6},\"concept\":\"market-participants\"},\"check\":[\"q-0.1.2-1\",\"q-0.1.2-2\",\"q-0.1.2-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Hedgers, investors, institutions, market makers, speculators and central banks all trade, each for its own reason.\",\"Every trade has a counterparty with its own horizon, need and information. The chart doesn't show who.\",\"Across from you are full-time firms with better tools and lower costs. Plan with that in mind.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 0.1.3: the markets they trade in, from stocks to crypto, and how their hours and costs differ.\"}},\"0.1.3\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.3\",\"bridge\":\"In 0.1.2 you met who trades and why. Here you tour where they trade: stocks, forex, futures, CFDs, bonds and crypto, and what carries over from LABX, the instrument you practise on.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"It's Saturday. Which of these can you trade right now?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"shares\",\"label\":\"Shares on a stock exchange\"},{\"id\":\"futures\",\"label\":\"A futures contract\"},{\"id\":\"eurusd\",\"label\":\"EUR/USD\"},{\"id\":\"bitcoin\",\"label\":\"Bitcoin\"}],\"answer\":\"bitcoin\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. Only Bitcoin: crypto trades 24/7. Forex and most futures pause from Friday evening to Sunday evening, and stock exchanges keep weekday sessions.\",\"concept\":\"asset-classes\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Families of instruments\",\"text\":\"An [[asset-class]] is a family of instruments with its own rules, hours and costs. The main ones: [[stock|stocks]], [[etf|ETFs]] that track an [[index]], [[forex]], [[futures]] on a [[commodity]] or an index, [[cryptocurrency]], [[bond|bonds]], and [[derivative|derivatives]] such as [[cfd|CFDs]] and options.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Hours open in a normal week\",\"text\":\"Fewer hours means more time when price can move while you can't trade. When trading reopens, price can jump: a [[gap]] past your stop, the price where you planned to get out.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"unit\":\" h\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"Crypto spot\",\"value\":168},{\"label\":\"Forex\",\"value\":120},{\"label\":\"Futures (daily break)\",\"value\":115},{\"label\":\"US stocks, main session\",\"value\":32.5}],\"steps\":[\"Crypto spot never closes: 24 hours × 7 days = 168.\",\"Forex runs 24 hours a day from Sunday evening to Friday evening: 120.\",\"Futures trade nearly around the clock, Sunday to Friday, with a one-hour daily break: about 115.\",\"A US exchange's main session is 6.5 hours a day: 32.5 a week. Prices can gap overnight.\"]},\"concept\":\"asset-classes\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Leverage, costs and who's across\",\"text\":\"Classes differ in [[leverage]] (borrowing to trade bigger) too. Since 2018, EU retail CFD leverage has been capped between 30:1 and 2:1 by asset. Costs differ: futures add a roll, shorting stocks (betting on a fall) needs borrowed shares, CFDs charge overnight funding. And so does the counterparty: an exchange's clearing house for futures, the provider itself for a CFD.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"One feature each\",\"prompt\":\"Match each feature to the asset class it describes.\",\"columns\":[\"Feature\",\"Asset class\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Expires and must be rolled\",\"right\":\"Futures\"},{\"left\":\"Quoted as a pair, one currency against another\",\"right\":\"Forex\"},{\"left\":\"Trades 24/7\",\"right\":\"Crypto\"},{\"left\":\"Shorting needs borrowed shares\",\"right\":\"Stocks\"},{\"left\":\"You never own the asset; the provider is your counterparty\",\"right\":\"CFDs\"},{\"left\":\"Price falls when interest rates rise\",\"right\":\"Bonds\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. One feature is often enough to tell the classes apart, and each one changes how you trade it.\",\"concept\":\"asset-classes\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"What carries over from LABX\",\"prompt\":\"LABX is one synthetic instrument. Sort each item: does it work the same on any instrument, or does it change from one class to the next?\",\"columns\":[\"Item\",\"On a real instrument\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Buying at the ask, selling at the bid\",\"right\":\"Works the same\"},{\"left\":\"Deciding the risk per trade first\",\"right\":\"Works the same\"},{\"left\":\"A stop with every entry, and sizing from it\",\"right\":\"Works the same\"},{\"left\":\"Trading hours\",\"right\":\"Changes\"},{\"left\":\"Leverage limits\",\"right\":\"Changes\"},{\"left\":\"Expiry and roll\",\"right\":\"Changes\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. The habits transfer; the numbers change. Real instruments get their own units later in the course.\",\"concept\":\"asset-classes\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"info\",\"text\":\"LABX is synthetic: one liquid instrument, a fixed 0.10 spread, no expiry and no leverage. The habits transfer; the numbers change.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"stocks\",\"label\":\"Stocks\"},{\"id\":\"etf-index\",\"label\":\"ETFs and indices\"},{\"id\":\"forex\",\"label\":\"Forex\"},{\"id\":\"futures\",\"label\":\"Futures\"},{\"id\":\"cfd\",\"label\":\"CFDs\"},{\"id\":\"bonds\",\"label\":\"Bonds\"},{\"id\":\"crypto\",\"label\":\"Crypto\"},{\"id\":\"options\",\"label\":\"Options\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"eurusd\",\"text\":\"EUR/USD\",\"answer\":\"forex\",\"correct\":\"Forex. A pair: how many US dollars one euro costs. It trades 24 hours a day, Sunday evening to Friday evening.\",\"rationales\":{\"stocks\":\"A stock is a share of one company, traded on an exchange. Here two currencies are quoted as a pair: euros priced in dollars.\",\"etf-index\":\"An ETF is a fund holding a basket, often tracking an index. Here two currencies are quoted as a pair: euros priced in dollars.\",\"futures\":\"A futures contract trades on an exchange and expires on a set date. Here two currencies are quoted as a pair: euros priced in dollars.\",\"cfd\":\"A CFD is a provider's contract on a price change; you never own the asset. Here two currencies are quoted as a pair: euros priced in dollars.\",\"bonds\":\"A bond is a loan that pays interest. Here two currencies are quoted as a pair: euros priced in dollars.\",\"crypto\":\"Crypto is a digital asset that trades around the clock. Here two currencies are quoted as a pair: euros priced in dollars.\",\"options\":\"An option is a right, not a duty, to buy or sell at a fixed price. Here two currencies are quoted as a pair: euros priced in dollars.\"}},{\"id\":\"gold\",\"text\":\"One gold futures contract\",\"answer\":\"futures\",\"correct\":\"Futures. Gold is the commodity; the contract expires, so keeping the position means rolling it to the next contract.\",\"rationales\":{\"stocks\":\"A stock is a share of one company, traded on an exchange. Here gold is traded through an exchange contract with an expiry date.\",\"etf-index\":\"An ETF is a fund holding a basket, often tracking an index. Here gold is traded through an exchange contract with an expiry date.\",\"forex\":\"Forex is one currency quoted against another. Here gold is traded through an exchange contract with an expiry date.\",\"cfd\":\"Both pay on gold's price, but this one is an exchange contract that expires. A CFD is a deal with a provider, with no expiry.\",\"bonds\":\"A bond is a loan that pays interest. Here gold is traded through an exchange contract with an expiry date.\",\"crypto\":\"Crypto is a digital asset that trades around the clock. Here gold is traded through an exchange contract with an expiry date.\",\"options\":\"A futures contract binds both sides to trade. An option gives a right that its holder can choose not to use.\"}},{\"id\":\"shares\",\"text\":\"100 shares of a listed company\",\"answer\":\"stocks\",\"correct\":\"Stocks. You own a slice of one company. Selling it short would need borrowed shares.\",\"rationales\":{\"etf-index\":\"An ETF is a fund holding a basket, often tracking an index. Here you own part of one company, bought on its exchange.\",\"forex\":\"Forex is one currency quoted against another. Here you own part of one company, bought on its exchange.\",\"futures\":\"A futures contract trades on an exchange and expires on a set date. Here you own part of one company, bought on its exchange.\",\"cfd\":\"A CFD is a provider's contract on a price change; you never own the asset. Here you own part of one company, bought on its exchange.\",\"bonds\":\"A bond is a loan that pays interest. Here you own part of one company, bought on its exchange.\",\"crypto\":\"Crypto is a digital asset that trades around the clock. Here you own part of one company, bought on its exchange.\",\"options\":\"An option is a right, not a duty, to buy or sell at a fixed price. Here you own part of one company, bought on its exchange.\"}},{\"id\":\"bitcoin\",\"text\":\"Bitcoin on an exchange\",\"answer\":\"crypto\",\"correct\":\"Crypto. It trades around the clock, even on a Saturday.\",\"rationales\":{\"stocks\":\"A stock is a share of one company, traded on an exchange. Here a digital asset trades on an exchange, 24/7.\",\"etf-index\":\"An ETF is a fund holding a basket, often tracking an index. Here a digital asset trades on an exchange, 24/7.\",\"forex\":\"Forex is one currency quoted against another. Here a digital asset trades on an exchange, 24/7.\",\"futures\":\"A futures contract trades on an exchange and expires on a set date. Here a digital asset trades on an exchange, 24/7.\",\"cfd\":\"A CFD is a provider's contract on a price change; you never own the asset. Here a digital asset trades on an exchange, 24/7.\",\"bonds\":\"A bond is a loan that pays interest. Here a digital asset trades on an exchange, 24/7.\",\"options\":\"An option is a right, not a duty, to buy or sell at a fixed price. Here a digital asset trades on an exchange, 24/7.\"}},{\"id\":\"right\",\"text\":\"The right to buy a share at a fixed price before a date\",\"answer\":\"options\",\"correct\":\"Options. A derivative, like futures and CFDs. Options come much later in the course.\",\"rationales\":{\"stocks\":\"It's about a share, but you don't own the share: you own the right to buy it. That's an option.\",\"etf-index\":\"An ETF is a fund holding a basket, often tracking an index. Here you hold a right, not a duty, to buy at a fixed price before a date.\",\"forex\":\"Forex is one currency quoted against another. Here you hold a right, not a duty, to buy at a fixed price before a date.\",\"futures\":\"Futures oblige both sides to trade. This is a right you can choose not to use: an option.\",\"cfd\":\"A CFD pays every price change and never expires. This is a right to buy at a fixed price before a date: an option.\",\"bonds\":\"A bond is a loan that pays interest. Here you hold a right, not a duty, to buy at a fixed price before a date.\",\"crypto\":\"Crypto is a digital asset that trades around the clock. Here you hold a right, not a duty, to buy at a fixed price before a date.\"}},{\"id\":\"fund\",\"text\":\"A fund that tracks 500 large companies\",\"answer\":\"etf-index\",\"correct\":\"ETFs and indices. One share of the fund gives you a slice of the whole basket.\",\"rationales\":{\"stocks\":\"It trades like a share, but it holds 500 companies at once: a fund tracking an index, an ETF.\",\"forex\":\"Forex is one currency quoted against another. Here one fund holds a basket that follows an index of 500 companies.\",\"futures\":\"A futures contract trades on an exchange and expires on a set date. Here one fund holds a basket that follows an index of 500 companies.\",\"cfd\":\"A CFD is a provider's contract on a price change; you never own the asset. Here one fund holds a basket that follows an index of 500 companies.\",\"bonds\":\"A bond is a loan that pays interest. Here one fund holds a basket that follows an index of 500 companies.\",\"crypto\":\"Crypto is a digital asset that trades around the clock. Here one fund holds a basket that follows an index of 500 companies.\",\"options\":\"An option is a right, not a duty, to buy or sell at a fixed price. Here one fund holds a basket that follows an index of 500 companies.\"}},{\"id\":\"govt\",\"text\":\"A government bond paying a coupon\",\"answer\":\"bonds\",\"correct\":\"Bonds. A loan that pays interest, the coupon. When interest rates rise, its price falls.\",\"rationales\":{\"stocks\":\"A stock is a share of one company, traded on an exchange. Here you lend to a government and receive regular interest, the coupon.\",\"etf-index\":\"An ETF is a fund holding a basket, often tracking an index. Here you lend to a government and receive regular interest, the coupon.\",\"forex\":\"Forex is one currency quoted against another. Here you lend to a government and receive regular interest, the coupon.\",\"futures\":\"A futures contract trades on an exchange and expires on a set date. Here you lend to a government and receive regular interest, the coupon.\",\"cfd\":\"A CFD is a provider's contract on a price change; you never own the asset. Here you lend to a government and receive regular interest, the coupon.\",\"crypto\":\"Crypto is a digital asset that trades around the clock. Here you lend to a government and receive regular interest, the coupon.\",\"options\":\"An option is a right, not a duty, to buy or sell at a fixed price. Here you lend to a government and receive regular interest, the coupon.\"}},{\"id\":\"index-cfd\",\"text\":\"A CFD on a stock index\",\"answer\":\"cfd\",\"correct\":\"CFDs. The index is only what the price tracks. Your counterparty is the provider, not an exchange.\",\"rationales\":{\"stocks\":\"A stock is a share of one company, traded on an exchange. Here you hold a contract with a provider that pays the index's price change.\",\"etf-index\":\"The index is only what the price follows. You hold a contract with a provider, not a fund: that's a CFD.\",\"forex\":\"Forex is one currency quoted against another. Here you hold a contract with a provider that pays the index's price change.\",\"futures\":\"A futures contract trades on an exchange and expires on a set date. Here you hold a contract with a provider that pays the index's price change.\",\"bonds\":\"A bond is a loan that pays interest. Here you hold a contract with a provider that pays the index's price change.\",\"crypto\":\"Crypto is a digital asset that trades around the clock. Here you hold a contract with a provider that pays the index's price change.\",\"options\":\"An option is a right, not a duty, to buy or sell at a fixed price. Here you hold a contract with a provider that pays the index's price change.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Eight products. Read each card and name its asset class.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":8},\"correct\":\"All eight sorted. Each class brings its own hours, costs and counterparty, so check them before you trade any of them.\",\"hints\":[\"Look for the giveaway: a currency pair, an expiry date, a provider contract, a coupon, or trading 24/7.\",\"Futures oblige both sides to trade at a set date; an option is only a right; a CFD is a deal with a provider.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":8},\"concept\":\"asset-classes\"},\"check\":[\"q-0.1.3-1\",\"q-0.1.3-2\",\"q-0.1.3-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Stocks, ETFs, forex, futures, CFDs, bonds and crypto each have their own rules, hours and costs.\",\"Classes differ in trading hours, leverage, costs, gap risk and who your counterparty is.\",\"From LABX the habits transfer: bid and ask, costs, stops, sizing. Hours, leverage and expiry change.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 0.1.4: what any trading result in these markets has to beat: buy-and-hold, after costs.\"}},\"0.1.4\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.4\",\"bridge\":\"In 0.1.3 you saw that each asset class has its own hours and costs. Here those costs meet a benchmark: buying and holding the same market, which every trading result has to beat.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Over one year, Dana traded LABX 40 times and finished +6.0% after costs. [[buy-and-hold|Buying on day one and holding]] finished +9.0%. Who did better?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"dana\",\"label\":\"Dana: she was active and profitable\"},{\"id\":\"hold\",\"label\":\"Buy-and-hold\"},{\"id\":\"same\",\"label\":\"The same\"}],\"answer\":\"hold\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. Buy-and-hold did better by 3.0 points, with no effort. A full comparison would also weigh the risk taken, which comes later.\",\"concept\":\"passive-benchmark\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Two ways to use a market\",\"text\":\"**Investing** means holding assets for their return over years: dividends, coupons and growth, with few trades. **Trading** seeks profit from price changes over shorter horizons, and only works with a specific edge, an advantage that pays after costs. Investing earns what the market pays. Trading adds value only if it beats that.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"How long do you hold?\",\"prompt\":\"Trading styles differ mainly in how long a position stays open. Match each holding period to its style.\",\"columns\":[\"Holding period\",\"Style\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Seconds to minutes, many trades a day\",\"right\":\"Scalping\"},{\"left\":\"Minutes to hours, flat by the close\",\"right\":\"Day trading\"},{\"left\":\"Two days to a few weeks\",\"right\":\"Swing trading\"},{\"left\":\"Weeks to months\",\"right\":\"Position trading\"},{\"left\":\"Years\",\"right\":\"Investing\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. The shorter the hold, the more trades a week, and the more often you pay the spread and the commission.\",\"concept\":\"trading-styles\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Costs decide the game\",\"text\":\"[[scalping|Scalpers]] and [[day-trading|day traders]] pay costs many times a week; [[swing-trading|swing]] and [[position-trading|position traders]] far less often. Before costs, trading is close to zero-sum around the market's own return. After costs, it's negative-sum for the median trader. Costs are certain; an edge is not.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"The benchmark ghost\",\"text\":\"The ghost is your [[benchmark]]: the same market, bought and held, after costs. A trading result only counts as good if it beats it.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":0,\"h\":0,\"l\":0,\"c\":0},{\"o\":0.8,\"h\":0.8,\"l\":0.8,\"c\":0.8},{\"o\":1.5,\"h\":1.5,\"l\":1.5,\"c\":1.5},{\"o\":1.1,\"h\":1.1,\"l\":1.1,\"c\":1.1},{\"o\":2.4,\"h\":2.4,\"l\":2.4,\"c\":2.4},{\"o\":3.2,\"h\":3.2,\"l\":3.2,\"c\":3.2},{\"o\":2.9,\"h\":2.9,\"l\":2.9,\"c\":2.9},{\"o\":4.1,\"h\":4.1,\"l\":4.1,\"c\":4.1},{\"o\":5,\"h\":5,\"l\":5,\"c\":5},{\"o\":5.8,\"h\":5.8,\"l\":5.8,\"c\":5.8},{\"o\":6.9,\"h\":6.9,\"l\":6.9,\"c\":6.9},{\"o\":8.1,\"h\":8.1,\"l\":8.1,\"c\":8.1},{\"o\":9,\"h\":9,\"l\":9,\"c\":9}],\"range\":[-1,15],\"height\":260,\"describe\":\"One year of month-end results in percent. Buy-and-hold rises to +9.0%. Dana's trading reaches +14.0% before costs but +6.0% after the costs of 40 round trips.\"},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"The ghost: buy LABX on day one and hold. After costs, +9.0% at year end.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"line\",\"values\":[0,0.8,1.5,1.1,2.4,3.2,2.9,4.1,5,5.8,6.9,8.1,9],\"role\":\"neutral\",\"dashed\":true,\"label\":\"GHOST\"}]},{\"text\":\"Dana's 40 trades before costs (GROSS): +14.0%. On paper, she beat the ghost.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"line\",\"values\":[0,1,2.6,3,4.2,5.5,6.1,7.4,8.8,10.1,11,12.6,14],\"role\":\"concept\",\"dashed\":true,\"label\":\"GROSS\"}]},{\"text\":\"Each round trip (buy, then sell) costs about 0.2%. Forty of them: 8.0 points. After costs (NET): +6.0%.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"line\",\"values\":[0,0.4,1.2,1,1.6,2.1,2.1,2.8,3.4,4.1,4.4,5.2,6],\"role\":\"accent\",\"label\":\"NET\"}]},{\"text\":\"After costs, Dana is 3.0 points behind the ghost.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"bracket\",\"from\":6,\"to\":9,\"index\":12,\"label\":\"−3.0\"}]}]},\"concept\":\"passive-benchmark\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The most active fifth\",\"lines\":[\"A study followed 66,465 US households, 1991–96.\",\"The most active fifth earned 11.4% a year.\",\"The market earned 17.9% a year.\",\"Gap = 17.9 − 11.4 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":6.5,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"points\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":29.3,\"text\":\"That adds the two. The gap is the difference: subtract 11.4 from 17.9.\"},{\"value\":-6.5,\"text\":\"Right size, wrong sign. The market earned more, so the gap in its favour is positive.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 6.5 points a year behind the market.\"},\"after\":\"6.5 points a year. Trading costs and frequency explained the gap, not the choice of stocks (Barber and Odean, 2000).\",\"concept\":\"passive-benchmark\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Every result in this course is shown after costs, next to the benchmark ghost. Beating it is the real test, and most active traders don't.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Your turn: trade down to the ghost\",\"prompt\":\"Before costs you made +10.0%; the ghost made +6.0%. Each round trip costs about 0.2% of the account. How many trades pull your result down to the ghost's?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":0,\"max\":60,\"step\":1,\"start\":0,\"unit\":\"trades\",\"answer\":20,\"tolerance\":0,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":0,\"to\":19,\"text\":\"Not yet: below 20 trades, costs take less than 4.0 points, so you stay above the ghost. At 10 trades you're at +8.0%.\"},{\"from\":20,\"to\":20,\"text\":\"Right: 20 × 0.2 = 4.0 points of costs, and 10.0 − 4.0 = 6.0, level with the ghost.\"},{\"from\":21,\"to\":60,\"text\":\"Too many: past 20 trades, costs drop you below the ghost. At 30 trades you're at +4.0%.\"}]},\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"Before costs\",\"value\":10,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"Costs, 20 × 0.2\",\"value\":-4,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"After costs\",\"value\":6,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"Benchmark ghost\",\"value\":6,\"role\":\"neutral\"}],\"steps\":[\"Before costs, +10.0%. Twenty round trips at 0.2% each cost 4.0 points.\",\"After costs, +6.0%: exactly the ghost.\"]},\"reveal\":\"20 trades: the gap to close is 10.0 − 6.0 = 4.0 points, and 4.0 ÷ 0.2 = 20. About, ignoring compounding. The 0.2% is a position as big as the account, 99 units near 100.00, paying 19.81 in spread and commission per round trip.\",\"concept\":\"passive-benchmark\"}],\"check\":[\"q-0.1.4-1\",\"q-0.1.4-2\",\"q-0.1.4-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Investing holds for years with few trades; trading seeks short-term price changes and needs an edge.\",\"Compare any result with buy-and-hold on the same market, after costs: the benchmark ghost.\",\"Every round trip costs something, so the more you trade, the larger the edge you need.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 0.1.5: how most retail traders actually do against that benchmark, in the regulators' own numbers.\"}},\"0.1.5\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.5\",\"bridge\":\"In 0.1.4 you saw Dana beat the market before costs and trail it after. Here you see how whole populations of retail traders do, and the common reasons most of them lose.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Out of every 100 retail CFD accounts in the EU, how many lose money?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":0,\"max\":100,\"step\":1,\"start\":50,\"answer\":81.5,\"tolerance\":7.5},\"compare\":\"You guessed {yours}. The national regulators' analyses cited by ESMA in 2018 found 74 to 89 out of every 100.\",\"concept\":\"retail-loss-rates\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"A number every provider must show\",\"text\":\"In the EU and the UK, CFD providers must state what share of their retail accounts lose money, in a standard [[risk-warning]]. The figures come from [[regulator|regulators]] and from the providers' own client data. They are a base rate: what usually happens to people like you, not anyone's opinion.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"What the regulators and studies found\",\"text\":\"These are whole populations, not a few unlucky traders. The Brazil figure counts only those who kept going; the study found no sign that they learned to win.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"EU (ESMA), low end\",\"value\":74,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"EU (ESMA), high end\",\"value\":89,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"UK (FCA), 2016 sample\",\"value\":82,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"France (AMF), 2009–12\",\"value\":89,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"Brazil, 300+ days of day trading\",\"value\":97,\"role\":\"down\"}],\"steps\":[\"ESMA, 2018: in national analyses, at least 74% of retail CFD accounts lost money.\",\"And up to 89%, with average losses of €1,600 to €29,000 per client.\",\"UK: in the FCA's 2016 sample of CFD accounts, 82% lost money.\",\"France, 2009–2012: about 89% of 14,799 active clients lost, about €10,900 each on average.\",\"Brazil: 97% of those who day traded futures for 300+ days lost. Taiwan: under 1% reliably profit.\"]},\"concept\":\"retail-loss-rates\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Why most fail, and where this course answers\",\"prompt\":\"Each common reason for failing has a unit of this course built against it. Match them.\",\"columns\":[\"Why traders fail\",\"Where you'll work on it\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"No stop, and too big a size\",\"right\":\"1.4 Risk comes first\"},{\"left\":\"Costs eaten by many small trades\",\"right\":\"1.1 Prices, quotes and costs\"},{\"left\":\"Doubling the size after losses\",\"right\":\"1.8 Danger zone\"},{\"left\":\"No plan and no review\",\"right\":\"1.6 Your first plan and journal\"},{\"left\":\"A revenge trade after a loss\",\"right\":\"1.7 The trader's mind I\"},{\"left\":\"Judging a method on a few trades\",\"right\":\"0.2 Thinking in probabilities\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. None of these is about finding a magic setup. They're about risk, costs, behaviour and evidence.\",\"concept\":\"retail-loss-rates\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Good results here\",\"prompt\":\"You finish this course with great results in the simulator. What does that tell you about trading real money?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"ready\",\"label\":\"That I'm ready\",\"feedback\":\"Skills, yes. But a synthetic market without real fear can't show how you'd trade your own money.\"},{\"id\":\"little\",\"label\":\"Very little on its own\",\"feedback\":\"Right. It shows you can follow a process here. It doesn't prove profits with real money.\"},{\"id\":\"edge\",\"label\":\"That I've found an edge\",\"feedback\":\"A good run over a small sample can easily be luck. An edge needs far more evidence, after costs.\"}],\"answer\":\"little\"},\"reveal\":\"Very little on its own. The market here is synthetic, there's no real fear of losing your own money, and the sample is small. A simulator builds skills; it doesn't prove profits.\",\"concept\":\"when-not-to-trade\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Not trading is a valid result\",\"text\":\"Deciding not to day trade with real money, or to invest passively instead, is a valid result of this course. The goal here is competence in risk and in decisions under uncertainty. That carries over to investing, to work and to everyday choices, whatever you decide about trading.\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"If you ever trade real money: only money you can afford to lose, and only through a regulated broker. Both come back later in the course.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"no_stop\",\"label\":\"Entering without a stop\"},{\"id\":\"oversized\",\"label\":\"Position too large\"},{\"id\":\"revenge_size_up\",\"label\":\"Sizing up after a loss\"},{\"id\":\"chased_entry\",\"label\":\"Chasing extended moves\"},{\"id\":\"overtrading\",\"label\":\"Trading too often\"},{\"id\":\"clean\",\"label\":\"Within the rules\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"t2\",\"text\":\"Trade 2: Lee buys 80 LABX at 100.00 with the stop at 95.00. That's 400.00 at risk before costs, 4% of the account.\",\"answer\":\"oversized\",\"correct\":\"Position too large. 80 × 5.00 = 400.00 at risk: four times the 1% limit.\",\"rationales\":{\"no_stop\":\"That tag is for an entry with no stop at all. Here 400.00 is at risk, four times the 100.00 limit.\",\"revenge_size_up\":\"That tag is for a bigger size right after a loss. Here 400.00 is at risk, four times the 100.00 limit.\",\"chased_entry\":\"That tag is for buying after a big run, outside the plan. Here 400.00 is at risk, four times the 100.00 limit.\",\"overtrading\":\"That tag is for many trades with no plan behind them. Here 400.00 is at risk, four times the 100.00 limit.\",\"clean\":\"Within the rules needs a stop, risk under 100.00 and a planned setup. Here 400.00 is at risk, four times the 100.00 limit.\"}},{\"id\":\"t4\",\"text\":\"Trade 4: Lee buys 20 LABX at 100.00 on a planned setup, stop at 98.00: 41.98 at risk with costs. The trade loses.\",\"answer\":\"clean\",\"correct\":\"Within the rules. A stop, risk under 100.00, a planned setup. It lost, and it was still a sound decision.\",\"rationales\":{\"no_stop\":\"That tag is for an entry with no stop at all. Here the stop, the size and the setup all follow the plan; only the result was bad.\",\"oversized\":\"That tag is for more at risk than the 100.00 limit. Here the stop, the size and the setup all follow the plan; only the result was bad.\",\"revenge_size_up\":\"That tag is for a bigger size right after a loss. Here the stop, the size and the setup all follow the plan; only the result was bad.\",\"chased_entry\":\"That tag is for buying after a big run, outside the plan. Here the stop, the size and the setup all follow the plan; only the result was bad.\",\"overtrading\":\"That tag is for many trades with no plan behind them. Here the stop, the size and the setup all follow the plan; only the result was bad.\"}},{\"id\":\"t5\",\"text\":\"Trade 5: five minutes after trade 4's loss, Lee buys 45 LABX instead of the usual 20, stop 2.00 away.\",\"answer\":\"revenge_size_up\",\"correct\":\"Sizing up after a loss. The risk, 94.46 with costs, is under the limit, but the size jumped because of the last loss.\",\"rationales\":{\"no_stop\":\"That tag is for an entry with no stop at all. Here the size more than doubles minutes after a loss.\",\"oversized\":\"45 × 2.00 = 90.00, or 94.46 with costs: still under 100.00. The problem is what changed the size: the loss five minutes earlier.\",\"chased_entry\":\"That tag is for buying after a big run, outside the plan. Here the size more than doubles minutes after a loss.\",\"overtrading\":\"That tag is for many trades with no plan behind them. Here the size more than doubles minutes after a loss.\",\"clean\":\"Within the rules needs a stop, risk under 100.00 and a planned setup. Here the size more than doubles minutes after a loss.\"}},{\"id\":\"t7\",\"text\":\"Trade 7: Lee buys 20 LABX at 100.00 on a planned pullback, with no stop: 'I'll watch it.'\",\"answer\":\"no_stop\",\"correct\":\"Entering without a stop. The loss has no limit, so the risk can't even be measured.\",\"rationales\":{\"oversized\":\"That tag is for more at risk than the 100.00 limit. Here there's no stop at all, so the loss has no limit.\",\"revenge_size_up\":\"That tag is for a bigger size right after a loss. Here there's no stop at all, so the loss has no limit.\",\"chased_entry\":\"That tag is for buying after a big run, outside the plan. Here there's no stop at all, so the loss has no limit.\",\"overtrading\":\"That tag is for many trades with no plan behind them. Here there's no stop at all, so the loss has no limit.\",\"clean\":\"Within the rules needs a stop, risk under 100.00 and a planned setup. Here there's no stop at all, so the loss has no limit.\"}},{\"id\":\"t9\",\"text\":\"Trade 9: LABX has run up 5% in three hours. Lee buys at the top of the run, with no setup from the plan.\",\"answer\":\"chased_entry\",\"correct\":\"Chasing extended moves. The move made the decision, not the plan.\",\"rationales\":{\"no_stop\":\"That tag is for an entry with no stop at all. Here Lee buys after a big run, without a planned setup.\",\"oversized\":\"That tag is for more at risk than the 100.00 limit. Here Lee buys after a big run, without a planned setup.\",\"revenge_size_up\":\"That tag is for a bigger size right after a loss. Here Lee buys after a big run, without a planned setup.\",\"overtrading\":\"It's one trade, not many. What's wrong is the entry: buying at the top of a 5% run, with no setup.\",\"clean\":\"Within the rules needs a stop, risk under 100.00 and a planned setup. Here Lee buys after a big run, without a planned setup.\"}},{\"id\":\"day12\",\"text\":\"Day 12: Lee makes 14 trades in four hours, none from the plan's setups, each one paying the spread.\",\"answer\":\"overtrading\",\"correct\":\"Trading too often. Fourteen round trips of costs, and no setup behind any of them.\",\"rationales\":{\"no_stop\":\"That tag is for an entry with no stop at all. Here there are 14 trades in four hours, none of them planned.\",\"oversized\":\"That tag is for more at risk than the 100.00 limit. Here there are 14 trades in four hours, none of them planned.\",\"revenge_size_up\":\"That tag is for a bigger size right after a loss. Here there are 14 trades in four hours, none of them planned.\",\"chased_entry\":\"That tag is for buying after a big run, outside the plan. Here there are 14 trades in four hours, none of them planned.\",\"clean\":\"Within the rules needs a stop, risk under 100.00 and a planned setup. Here there are 14 trades in four hours, none of them planned.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Lee's month in the simulator: a 10,000.00 account and a 1% limit, 100.00 per trade. Name the failure on each card, or mark it within the rules.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":6},\"correct\":\"All six tagged. Five of the most common failures, and one losing trade that broke no rule. The result alone doesn't tell them apart.\",\"hints\":[\"Start with the easy one: which trade had no stop at all?\",\"Compare each size with the one before it, and check whether a loss came just before.\",\"A trade can lose and still be within the rules. Check the stop, the risk and the setup, not the result.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":6},\"concept\":\"retail-loss-rates\"},\"check\":[\"q-0.1.5-1\",\"q-0.1.5-2\",\"q-0.1.5-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"In the EU, 74% to 89% of retail CFD accounts lost money, per national regulators (ESMA, 2018).\",\"Most failures are no stop, oversizing, costs, revenge and small samples. Each has a unit here.\",\"Deciding not to trade real money, or to invest passively, is a valid result of this course.\"],\"next\":\"Next, 0.1.6: how this course and its simulator work, built as an answer to these numbers.\"}},\"0.1.6\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.6\",\"bridge\":\"In 0.1.5 you matched the common reasons traders fail to units of this course. Here you see how the course and the simulator work: the LABX world, the three scores and the evidence grades.\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Your Arena account grows from 10,000.00 to 12,000.00 vUSD in one week. Which score does that raise?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"dq\",\"label\":\"Decision Quality\"},{\"id\":\"rd\",\"label\":\"Risk Discipline\"},{\"id\":\"outcome\",\"label\":\"Only Simulated Outcome\"}],\"answer\":\"outcome\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. Only Simulated Outcome rises. A profit alone doesn't show whether your decisions or your risk control were any good.\",\"concept\":\"process-scores\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"The simulated world\",\"text\":\"You trade **LABX**, a synthetic instrument, in **vUSD**, from a 10,000.00 account. Each candle (chart bar) is one hour. The [[spread]] is 0.10: bid = mid − 0.05, ask = mid + 0.05. Every fill pays 0.05% [[commission]], a broker fee, rounded up to the cent. Risk is 1% per trade in missions and 2% in the Arena. No leverage.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Three scores, kept apart\",\"text\":\"[[decision-quality|Decision Quality]] grades your reasoning, given what you knew then. [[risk-discipline|Risk Discipline]] grades your risk rules: a stop on every entry, risk within the limit, no widened stop, no bigger size after a loss. [[simulated-outcome|Simulated Outcome]] is your simulated profit or loss: always shown, never graded.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Which score moves?\",\"prompt\":\"Match each action to the score it feeds.\",\"columns\":[\"Action\",\"Score\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Write the reason for the trade before entering\",\"right\":\"Decision Quality\"},{\"left\":\"Place a stop with every entry\",\"right\":\"Risk Discipline\"},{\"left\":\"Price happens to rise after you buy\",\"right\":\"Simulated Outcome\"},{\"left\":\"Skip a setup that breaks your rules\",\"right\":\"Decision Quality\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. Everything you control feeds the two graded scores. What price does next only moves the outcome.\",\"concept\":\"process-scores\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Evidence grades\",\"text\":\"Every method in this course carries an [[evidence-grade]]. **Mechanic**: true by construction, like the spread. **A**: robust across markets and decades. **B**: supported, but weaker since published or only in some regimes. **C**: mixed. **D**: unproven when tested. **F**: loses by design. Even an A shrinks once it's known.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"In the original studies\",\"value\":100,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"Out of sample\",\"value\":74,\"role\":\"concept\"},{\"label\":\"After publication\",\"value\":42,\"role\":\"neutral\"}],\"steps\":[\"97 published return predictors. Their returns in the original studies: call that 100.\",\"On data outside the studies: 26% less.\",\"After publication, once everyone could read them: 58% less (McLean and Pontiff, 2016).\"]},\"concept\":\"evidence-grades\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Grade the claim\",\"prompt\":\"Match each claim to its evidence grade.\",\"columns\":[\"Claim\",\"Grade\"],\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"A round trip pays the spread\",\"right\":\"Mechanic\"},{\"left\":\"Diversified trend following over decades\",\"right\":\"A\"},{\"left\":\"Stops cluster just beyond round numbers\",\"right\":\"B\"},{\"left\":\"Elliott waves predict turning points\",\"right\":\"D\"},{\"left\":\"Doubling your size after each loss\",\"right\":\"F\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. A grade tells you how well an idea has held up when tested, not that it will work for you.\",\"concept\":\"evidence-grades\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Results here are always after costs and next to the benchmark ghost. Each lesson runs Hook, Learn, Try, Check, Recap; missed questions come back for review, and nothing is locked.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Your turn: check the ticket\",\"lines\":[\"Buy 40 LABX at 100.00, stop at 98.00.\",\"Loss at the stop: 40 × 2.00 = 80.00.\",\"Commission in: 40 × 100.00 × 0.05% = 2.00.\",\"Commission out: 40 × 98.00 × 0.05% = 1.96.\",\"At risk: 80.00 + 2.00 + 1.96 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":83.96,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":80,\"text\":\"That's the price move only. Add both commissions: 2.00 in and 1.96 out.\"},{\"value\":82,\"text\":\"You added one commission. The exit pays one too: 1.96.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 83.96, under the 1% limit of 100.00.\"},\"after\":\"83.96 is under the 100.00 limit. With the stop on, the risk is defined and within the rule.\",\"concept\":\"process-scores\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Your turn: fix the ticket\",\"prompt\":\"This ticket has the stop switched off and an empty Reason field. Which changes earn full Risk Discipline and give Decision Quality something to grade?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"both\",\"label\":\"Stop on at 98.00, and a reason written before entry\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The stop defines the risk, 83.96, and the reason gives your decision something to be judged on.\"},{\"id\":\"wait\",\"label\":\"Stop on at 98.00, then wait to see if it wins\",\"feedback\":\"The stop covers Risk Discipline, but Decision Quality needs your reason, written before you enter. Winning doesn't supply it.\"},{\"id\":\"reason\",\"label\":\"No stop, but a strong written reason\",\"feedback\":\"A reason helps Decision Quality, but without a stop the risk has no limit, so Risk Discipline can't be full.\"},{\"id\":\"after\",\"label\":\"A reason written after the trade closes in profit\",\"feedback\":\"Written afterwards, a reason only describes the result. And without a stop, the risk was never limited.\"}],\"answer\":\"both\"},\"reveal\":\"A stop at 98.00 puts 83.96 at risk, within the 100.00 limit, and a reason written before entry is what Decision Quality grades. Whether the trade then wins or loses moves only Simulated Outcome.\",\"concept\":\"process-scores\"}],\"check\":[\"q-0.1.6-1\",\"q-0.1.6-2\",\"q-0.1.6-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"LABX, vUSD, a 10,000.00 account, a 0.10 spread and 0.05% commission per fill. No leverage.\",\"Decision Quality and Risk Discipline grade your process. Simulated Outcome shows profit but is never graded.\",\"An evidence grade says how well an idea held up when tested, not that it will work.\"],\"next\":\"Next comes the Unit 0.1 checkpoint, then unit 0.2, Thinking in probabilities, starting with process versus outcome.\"}}}");
